  · Write {"file_path":"/Users/kevin.hartman/code/stockflow-sprint2-design-ready/.consort/features/F6-split-tracking-code/architecture.json","content":"{\n  \"feature_id\": \"F6-split-tracking-code\",\n  \"service_backed\": true,\n  \"layers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"api\",\n      \"role\": \"boundary\",\n      \"module\": \"app/routes/\",\n      \"may_import\": [\"service\"],\n      \"renders_via\": \"react\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"services\",\n      \"role\": \"service\",\n      \"module\": \"app/services/\",\n      \"may_import\": [\"repository\", \"models\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"repositories\",\n      \"role\": \"repository\",\n      \"module\": \"app/repositories/\",\n      \"may_import\": [\"models\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"models\",\n      \"role\": \"models\",\n      \"module\": \"app/models/\",\n      \"may_import\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"persistence_invariants\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PI1-expand-migration-reversible\",\n      \"type\": \"migration_reversible\",\n      \"table\": \"stock_records\",\n      \"brief\": \"Additive migration on the pre-existing stock_records table: it adds nullable batch_number + serial_number. A single-step downgrade drops ONLY those two columns while every pre-existing row persists, and a re-upgrade re-adds them (additive round-trip with row preservation, since the table pre-exists from F1).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PI2-expand-migration-atomic\",\n      \"type\": \"transactional\",\n      \"table\": \"stock_records\",\n      \"brief\": \"The expand migration adds the two columns and backfills them from inventory_code within one transaction: either every pre-existing row is present with columns added and conforming rows backfilled, or the migration does not commit — no torn/partial state and no dropped row (realizes R1 at the migration boundary).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"open_decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"OD1-inventory-code-composition\",\n      \"question\": \"What composes the combined inventory_code, and is its leading segment authoritative for location? (F1 deferred this as OD2-inventory-code-composition.)\",\n      \"decision_status\": \"resolved\",\n      \"resolved_by_story\": \"S1-expand-schema\",\n      \"resolution\": \"inventory_code is a hyphen-delimited location-batch-serial string of VARIABLE width (split on '-': segment 2 = batch_number, segment 3 = serial_number; codes like 'X-1' or a bare 'c' have no batch/serial segment). The leading segment is NOT authoritative for location — the canonical location column (part of UNIQUE(sku,location) from F1) stays unchanged (AC4). Consistent with F1, which already stores location as its own column; no contradiction with any gated F1 AC.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"OD2-nonconforming-remediation\",\n      \"question\": \"Rows whose inventory_code does not parse are left with NULL batch_number/serial_number (AC2). Do these rows require a remediation/backfill-correction path BEFORE the S2 contract story drops inventory_code (which permanently discards the original code, the only source of the unparsed batch/serial), or is a NULL-forever state acceptable? The integrity-probe count (AC3) exists precisely to gate that decision.\",\n      \"decision_status\": \"deferred\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nfrs\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NFR-R1-migration-data-survival\",\n      \"brief_ref\": \"R1\",\n      \"applies_to\": \"F6-split-tracking-code\",\n      \"category\": \"data-integrity\",\n      \"tier\": \"product\",\n      \"hil_status\": \"proposed\",\n      \"fitness_functions\": [\n        \"the F6 expand migration (adding batch_number + serial_number and backfilling from inventory_code) preserves every pre-existing stock_records row: post-migration the row count is unchanged and no row is dropped or corrupted (R1, AC2).\",\n        \"the expand migration is reversible as a round-trip: a single-step downgrade drops only the two added columns while every pre-existing row persists, and a re-upgrade re-adds them (PI1).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NFR-R2-preserve-non-negative\",\n      \"brief_ref\": \"R2\",\n      \"applies_to\": \"F6-split-tracking-code\",\n      \"category\": \"data-integrity\",\n      \"tier\": \"product\",\n      \"hil_status\": \"proposed\",\n      \"fitness_function\": \"the F6 expand migration weakens no existing write-time guarantee: after migrating, the CHECK(quantity >= 0) on stock_records still rejects a negative quantity and the no-overcommit rule is untouched (F6 adds no write/pick path; it must only preserve R2, not re-implement it).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NFR-R3-preserve-unique-sku-location\",\n      \"brief_ref\": \"R3\",\n      \"applies_to\": \"F6-split-tracking-code\",\n      \"category\": \"data-integrity\",\n      \"tier\": \"product\",\n      \"hil_status\": \"proposed\",\n      \"fitness_function\": \"the F6 migration preserves UNIQUE(sku, location): after adding batch_number/serial_number and backfilling, no two rows share (sku, location) and a duplicate write is still rejected. Batch and serial become separately addressable WITHOUT altering the canonical (sku, location) identity (AC4).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NFR-R4-real-branch-integration-tests\",\n      \"brief_ref\": \"R4\",\n      \"applies_to\": \"F6-split-tracking-code\",\n      \"category\": \"operability\",\n      \"tier\": \"platform\",\n      \"hil_status\": \"proposed\",\n      \"fitness_function\": \"the F6 migration + integrity-probe suite runs against the paired Lakebase branch via DATABASE_URL (Alembic applied to the branch first), not a mock or in-memory substitute; CI refuses to merge a PR whose integration tests do not run against a real branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NFR-R5-spa-json-api-boundary\",\n      \"brief_ref\": \"R5\",\n      \"applies_to\": \"S3-split-fields-ui\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture-posture\",\n      \"tier\": \"platform\",\n      \"hil_status\": \"proposed\",\n      \"fitness_function\": \"the split batch_number and serial_number are exposed as distinct JSON fields by the boundary (not server-rendered HTML) and rendered as separately addressable fields in the single-page React + TypeScript client wherever the combined code was shown before.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NFR-config-in-env\",\n      \"applies_to\": \"F6-split-tracking-code\",\n      \"category\": \"config-in-env\",\n      \"tier\": \"platform\",\n      \"hil_status\": \"proposed\",\n      \"defended_by_gate\": \"config-in-env\",\n      \"fitness_function\": \"the migration and probe read the DB connection from DATABASE_URL in the environment (no hard-coded connection string; the substrate-provisioned databricks_postgres name is left untouched); enforced by the config-in-env gate.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"}
  · Write {"file_path":"/Users/kevin.hartman/code/stockflow-sprint2-design-ready/.consort/features/F6-split-tracking-code/architecture.md","content":"# F6 Split tracking code — Architecture (Architect Reviewer)\n\nExpand-then-contract schema refactor. **S1 (expand)** adds nullable `batch_number` +\n`serial_number` to the pre-existing `stock_records` table, backfills them from\n`inventory_code` by delimiter, and runs an integrity probe — all while `inventory_code`\nstill exists. S2 (contract) later drops `inventory_code`; S3 surfaces the split fields in\nthe SPA. This file is authored at S1; later stories append.\n\n`service_backed: true` — the feature persists data (a schema migration on `stock_records`).\nLayers reuse the project convention established by F1-stock-visibility (boundary=`app/routes/`\nreact, service=`app/services/`, repository=`app/repositories/`, models=`app/models/`); no\nlayer is remapped or renamed.\n\n## Layer assignment (S1)\n\nAll four S1 ACs are **Infra**: S1 is a pure schema/migration + data-store story with no HTTP\nor UI boundary. Each AC is a contract on the migrated `stock_records` shape/state, verified\nagainst the paired Lakebase branch. (The feature's E2E surface lives in S3-split-fields-ui,\nwhich renders the split fields in the SPA.)\n\n- AC1-backfill-conforming-code → **Infra** (backfill DDL + delimiter parse, owner: Alembic migration under `migrations/versions/`; nullable columns on `app/models/` stock model).\n- AC2-nonconforming-code-left-null → **Infra** (nonconforming code leaves both columns NULL, row survives; same migration).\n- AC3-integrity-probe-reports-count → **Infra** (read-only integrity probe over the table; owner: repository query in `app/repositories/` surfaced by a service reporting function in `app/services/`).\n- AC4-location-unchanged → **Infra** (migration touches only batch/serial; canonical `location` untouched).\n\n## Architectural Concerns Mapping\n\n| Concern | Owner layer / module | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Schema change + backfill (DDL/DML) | Alembic migration, `migrations/versions/` | Additive, transactional, reversible (PI1, PI2). |\n| ORM model shape (nullable columns) | models, `app/models/` (stock model) | `batch_number`/`serial_number` nullable — NULL is a valid state (AC2). |\n| Data-store access for the probe | repository, `app/repositories/` | Only layer touching the ORM/session; probe is a read query. |\n| Integrity-probe reporting | service, `app/services/` | Aggregates the nonconforming count; no raw SQL in boundary/domain. |\n| Data integrity / no silent loss (R1) | migration + tests | Every pre-existing row survives; unparsed codes → NULL, never guessed/dropped. |\n| Preserved existing constraints (R2, R3) | schema / migration | CHECK(quantity>=0) and UNIQUE(sku,location) must survive the refactor. |\n| Real-branch integration tests (R4) | test suite | Alembic applied to the paired branch; no mock/in-memory DB. |\n| Config in env | env / `DATABASE_URL` | Migration connects to substrate `databricks_postgres`; name untouched. |\n| Authn/authz | — | Out of bounds for V1; no actor identity assumed. |\n\n## Pattern proposals (SOLID module boundaries)\n\n- Keep DDL + backfill in the Alembic migration; keep the **integrity probe** as a read-only\n  repository query surfaced by a thin service reporting function — the probe is not a domain\n  behavior and must not leak SQL into the service/boundary.\n- Nullable columns on the models package (one module per aggregate) — the model change is\n  additive and does not alter the `(sku, location)` identity.\n\n## Risks\n\n- **Contract-drop data loss (S2):** dropping `inventory_code` in S2 permanently discards the\n  only source for rows the backfill could not parse (NULL batch/serial). The integrity-probe\n  count (AC3) is the gate on whether that loss is acceptable. Tracked as open decision\n  OD2-nonconforming-remediation; must be adjudicated before S2 is accepted.\n- **Variable-width parsing:** codes are variable width (`X-1`, bare `c`), not fixed. Backfill\n  must split on `-` and treat missing segments as NULL, never index blindly.\n- **Reversibility flavour:** S1's migration is ADDITIVE on a pre-existing table, so PI1 asserts\n  row-preservation on downgrade. The reversibility ROUND-TRIP that reconstructs `inventory_code`\n  is S2's (contract) concern, not S1's — do not conflate.\n\n## Decisions (for the PO at Gate 2)\n\n- **OD1-inventory-code-composition (RESOLVED by S1):** `inventory_code` = hyphen-delimited\n  `location-batch-serial`, variable width; segment 2 = batch, segment 3 = serial; leading\n  segment is NOT authoritative for `location` (canonical column stays). Resolves F1's deferred\n  OD2; consistent with F1's canonical `location` column — no contradiction with gated F1 ACs.\n  **Recommendation: accept.**\n- **OD2-nonconforming-remediation (DEFERRED):** whether NULL batch/serial rows need a\n  remediation path before S2 drops `inventory_code`. **Recommendation: defer to S2 review,\n  gated on the AC3 probe count** — proceed with S1 as specified regardless.\n\n## Test strategy\n\nReal integration tests against the paired Lakebase branch (pytest-bdd; Alembic applied to the\nbranch first; FK-aware targeted-DELETE cleanup). No mocks/stubs/in-memory DB (R4). ACs verified\nthrough this suite: AC1 (conforming backfill), AC2 (nonconforming → NULL + row survival),\nAC3 (integrity-probe count over mixed rows), AC4 (canonical location untouched). Persistence\ninvariants get a real-branch test each: PI1 (additive downgrade/upgrade round-trip preserving\nrows), PI2 (migration atomicity). NFR fitness tests: R1 row survival + reversibility, R2\npreserved non-negative CHECK, R3 preserved UNIQUE(sku,location).\n\n## Sign-off\n\n**Recommendation: proceed.** Layer assignments, layering (reused F1 convention), and NFR\ncoverage (R1–R5 + config-in-env) are in place; the one substantive open item\n(OD2-nonconforming-remediation) is correctly deferred to S2 and gated on the AC3 probe count.\n— Architect Reviewer\n"}
  · Bash {"command":"\\\n./scripts/lk consort-annotate-ac --feature F6-split-tracking-code --story S1-expand-schema --ac AC1-backfill-conforming-code --layer Infra --notes \"Infra: asserts the expand migration's backfill contract against the paired Lakebase branch — after adding batch_number/serial_number, a conforming location-batch-serial code's segment 2 lands in batch_number and segment 3 in serial_number. Owner: the Alembic migration under migrations/versions/ realizes the additive DDL + delimiter (split-on-'-') backfill; the stock model in app/models/ gains the two nullable columns and app/repositories/ is the only layer touching the ORM. Cross-cutting: data-integrity, no domain/parse logic in the boundary.\" && \\\n./scripts/lk consort-annotate-ac --feature F6-split-tracking-code --story S1-expand-schema --ac AC2-nonconforming-code-left-null --layer Infra --notes \"Infra: the backfill's nonconforming-code contract — a variable-width code lacking batch/serial segments (e.g. 'X-1', bare 'c') leaves both columns NULL (never guessed) and the row survives the migration with no data loss (R1). Verified against the real branch with variable-width fixtures. Owner: the same Alembic migration in migrations/versions/; the nullable batch_number/serial_number columns on the app/models/ stock model make NULL a valid state. Cross-cutting: data-integrity, no silent loss or corruption.\" && \\\n./scripts/lk consort-annotate-ac --feature F6-split-tracking-code --story S1-expand-schema --ac AC3-integrity-probe-reports-count --layer Infra --notes \"Infra: an integrity probe over the migrated stock_records table reports the aggregate count of nonconforming rows (those left with NULL batch_number and serial_number) for review before the change is accepted. Owner: a read-only query in app/repositories/ (the only layer touching the ORM session) surfaced by a thin reporting function in app/services/ — no raw SQL in the boundary or domain. Cross-cutting: observability/operability of the migration; verified against the paired branch over mixed conforming+nonconforming rows.\" && \\\n./scripts/lk consort-annotate-ac --feature F6-split-tracking-code --story S1-expand-schema --ac AC4-location-unchanged --layer Infra --notes \"Infra: asserts the migration preserves the canonical location column — the code's leading segment is NOT treated as authoritative, so location is left untouched even when it differs from that segment. Guards R3's (sku,location) identity through the refactor. Owner: the Alembic backfill migration in migrations/versions/ writes only batch_number/serial_number; the location column on the app/models/ stock model is unchanged. Cross-cutting: data-integrity, preservation of the existing UNIQUE(sku,location) constraint.\""}
